    "q-127324": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127324",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224656/assets/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
