    "q-127337": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127337",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\,{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224656/assets/b25d12781632bcc03576a79e7fbf1b22806fc68e3f521ecd602f1b0732d8d90d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{7}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-x)\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[0-((-1)^{2}y-(-1))]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-y-1)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-(1))-(-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
