    "q-127309": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127309",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224656/assets/f6e80e3010ec7c40d3f59dbb1da4e77c985c889a720b6f954177d8ad83a01351.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12yx,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
