    "q-127323": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127323",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224656/assets/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
